# What the kernel needs to be useful under `qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt`.
#
# Separate from `rivet.config` on purpose. That file is `ADR-024` §3's
# requirements — things the *product* depends on. This file is things the
# *development loop* depends on (`ADR-026`), and a future stripping pass
# (`ADR-024` §8, "strip when it works") needs to be able to see exactly what it
# would be dropping without reading a diff to find out.
#
# ## Why this is merged into the same kernel rather than a second one
#
# It would be tidier to keep a board kernel and a loop kernel apart. It would
# also be worse. Two kernel binaries is two configurations, and the loop's whole
# claim — `ADR-026` §2.1 — is that it runs *the kernel the hardware runs*, so
# that a difference in behaviour is a difference in Rivet and not in the
# kernel. A separate loop kernel would quietly reintroduce the failure §2.1
# describes: "the loop would then be testing the wrong thing while looking
# identical".
#
# So there is one Rivet kernel, and it carries virtio. The cost is a few tens
# of kilobytes of drivers for hardware the Pi does not have, which `ADR-024` §8
# is explicit about accepting during bring-up: "do not strip during bring-up".
#
# ## What this was measured against
#
# The stock Raspberry Pi kernel binds NOTHING under `-M virt`. Verified
# 2026-08-23 by booting it with three NIC configurations and reading the kernel
# log: `-device virtio-net-device` produced no virtio messages at all,
# `-device virtio-net-pci` produced nothing because there is no PCI host bridge,
# and `-device qemu-xhci -device usb-net` produced no xhci because qemu-xhci is
# itself a PCI device. Only `lan78xx` and `smsc95xx` registered, with no bus to
# find a device on. `tools/qemu/README.md` recorded the same wall from the other
# side: `strings` over the decompressed kernel8.img finds no `virtio` strings.
#
# That is what Stage 1 ran into, and it is what this file exists to remove.

# --- virtio, both transports ----------------------------------------------
# MMIO is what `-M virt` gives a `-device virtio-*-device`; PCI is what it gives
# a `-device virtio-*-pci`. Both are here because the failure mode of picking
# one is a device that QEMU creates, the guest never probes, and nothing
# anywhere reports as an error — the interface simply is not there.
CONFIG_VIRTIO=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_MENU=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO_CMDLINE_DEVICES=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI_LEGACY=y

# --- the PCI host bridge, without which virtio-net-pci is invisible -------
# `-M virt` exposes a generic ECAM host bridge described in the device tree.
# The stock Pi kernel has no driver for it, which is why the empirical probe saw
# only `PCI: CLS 0 bytes, default 64` — subsystem init with nothing behind it —
# and concluded there was no bridge. There is; nothing was driving it.
CONFIG_PCI=y
CONFIG_PCI_HOST_COMMON=y
CONFIG_PCI_HOST_GENERIC=y

# --- the network device the loop is actually for --------------------------
# `ADR-026` §7 open item 2. `ADR-026` §5 already ruled that this is the right
# trade: the netlink code under test is the same either way and only the driver
# differs, so virtio-net tests Rivet's Stage 1 code and settles nothing about
# `bcmgenet`. §4's table is where that distinction is written down.
CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET=y

# --- the rest of the virtio family, cheap and each earns its place --------
# BLK      — Stage 3's A/B image loop needs a virtual disk rather than an
#            initramfs (`ADR-026` §7 open item 3).
# CONSOLE  — a second console that is not the PL011, useful when the PL011 is
#            being used for something else.
# INPUT    — a keyboard. `tools/qemu/README.md` records that PID 1's shutdown
#            path is unreachable in this loop because `sendkey
#            ctrl-alt-delete` has no keyboard to arrive on: usb-kbd is behind
#            PCI and virtio-keyboard-device found no driver. This is the half of
#            that wall Rivet can remove.
CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_INPUT=y

# --- the interrupt controller `-M virt` actually presents ------------------
# QEMU's virt machine defaults to `gic-version=max`, which under TCG is GICv3.
# The Pi 4's own controller is a GIC-400, i.e. GICv2, so bcm2711_defconfig has
# no reason to carry v3 and the failure without it is a kernel that hangs before
# it can say why. Both are enabled; the kernel picks from the device tree.
CONFIG_ARM_GIC_V3=y
CONFIG_ARM_GIC_V3_ITS=y

# --- console and clock on the virt machine --------------------------------
# `-M virt` puts the console on a PL011 at ttyAMA0, which is also what the Pi's
# own primary UART is, so this is shared rather than emulator-specific. Asserted
# anyway: a kernel that boots to no console is the single most expensive failure
# in this loop, because it is indistinguishable from a hang.
CONFIG_SERIAL_AMBA_PL011=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_AMBA_PL011_CONSOLE=y
# PL031 is virt's RTC. Without it the guest's wall clock starts at the epoch,
# which makes every timestamp in the journal 1970 and every log correlation
# against the host useless.
CONFIG_RTC_DRV_PL031=y

# --- networking the loop's exit criterion needs ---------------------------
# Stage 1's exit criterion is reaching the machine over ethernet, and doing so under
# `-netdev user` means the guest must be able to hold an address and route.
# INET is in the base defconfig; these are named so a stripping pass sees them.
CONFIG_INET=y
CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST=y
CONFIG_UNIX=y

# --- a Bluetooth controller with no radio ----------------------------------
#
# `ADR-041` §7. `drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c` registers an HCI controller when
# a process opens `/dev/vhci` and writes `HCI_VENDOR_PKT`; the process on the
# other end of that descriptor **is** the controller. So the whole of
# `rivet-bluetoothd` — opening the adapter, the LE scan setup sequence, parsing
# an advertising report and handing it to an application — can be driven end to
# end in this loop with no hardware, by a test that composes the controller's
# answers itself.
#
# **This belongs here and not in `rivet.config`**, which is exactly the split
# `ADR-024` §5 rules and this file exists for. A virtual controller is a
# development-loop fact; a product kernel that carried it would be shipping a
# way to fabricate an adapter on a board.
#
# What it does NOT settle is in `ADR-026` §4's right-hand column and is stated
# plainly in `ADR-041` §8: whether the CYW43455 attaches at all. The study that
# preceded this measured `HCIDEVUP` timing out at 3 537 ms with
# `Bluetooth: hci1: Opcode 0x0c03 failed: -110` — the kernel sent a reset and
# nothing answered, because there is no radio. A real radio answers, and
# nothing in this file can stand in for one.
CONFIG_BT_HCIVHCI=y

# CONFIG_PACKET MOVED to linux/rivet.config on 2026-08-30, and the move is the
# whole point of this file being separate from that one. AF_PACKET is what a
# DHCP client needs to send a DISCOVER before the interface has an address --
# that is a PRODUCT requirement, not something the development loop happens to
# want, and ADR-024 §8's stripping pass exists to tell those two apart. Filed
# here it would have been stripped from a product kernel, and DHCP would have
# stopped working for a reason nothing named.
